@* One card tile. ShouldRender is overridden because Blazor treats a class-typed parameter as
   always-changed, which would re-render every visible tile on any state change.

   There is no drag handling here: touch's implicit pointer capture makes per-tile pointerenter
   unusable for sweeping. data-idx lets wwwroot/js/gridsweep.js hit-test with elementFromPoint
   instead, and it paints the .sel highlight directly so a sweep costs no Blazor renders.

   The tooltip is text only: a tile is the full card art, so an art preview on hover would show
   what is already on screen, while the tile has no room for the name, set or number. The list
   layout is the opposite case and does preview the art from its thumbnail. *@

@* Right-click removes a copy, the desktop counterpart to left-click adding one, without switching
   the whole grid into remove mode. The browser menu is suppressed on the tile only.

   A comment cannot live inside the attribute list below. Razor does not treat it as a comment
   there — it emits the text as an attribute name, and the browser throws
   "InvalidCharacterError: Element.setAttribute: Invalid attribute name" at render time, taking the
   whole grid down. It compiles, and bUnit's DOM is a parser rather than a browser, so it renders
   the nonsense without complaint; MarkupValidityTests scans every rendered page for names a
   browser would refuse.

   The tile is a cell of the grid its container declares, named by a sentence CardGrid builds so
   the keyboard cursor and the tile describe the card the same way. It carries no tabindex: the
   container holds the only tab stop and moves aria-activedescendant. *@
<div class="card-tile @(Count > 0 ? "owned" : "") @(Picked > 0 ? "picked" : "") @(Gold ? "flair gold-flair" : "")"
     data-idx="@Index" data-tip="@Tip"
     id="@DomId" role="gridcell" aria-label="@Label"
     @onclick="OnClick"
     @oncontextmenu="RightClick" @oncontextmenu:preventDefault="true"
     @onpointerdown="PointerDown" @onpointerup="PointerUp"
     @onpointercancel="CancelHold" @onpointerleave="CancelHold" @onpointermove="PointerMove">
    @* No rarity badge. The tile is the card art, which carries the rarity mark itself. The list
       layout does show rarity, because there the art is a 32px thumbnail. *@
    <img data-src="@Card.ArtUrl" alt="@Card.Name" decoding="async" draggable="false" />

    @* Detail needs its own target: a tap on the tile means "I own one more", so it cannot also
       mean "tell me about this". A corner button for a pointer, a long press for a finger. *@
    @if (ShowInfo)
    {
        @* Its context menu is stopped rather than prevented, so "open in new tab" still works on
           the one thing here that is genuinely a link. *@
        @* Out of the tab order: a focus stop per tile would put thousands of them between the grid
           and whatever follows it. The keyboard route to detail is `i` on the cursor, which
           gridkeys.js sends to KeyOpen. *@
        <a class="info" href="card/@Card.Key" title="Card detail" aria-label="Detail for @Card.Name"
           tabindex="-1"
           @onclick:stopPropagation="true" @onpointerdown:stopPropagation="true"
           @oncontextmenu:stopPropagation="true">info</a>
    }

    @* Wanting a card and owning it are different facts, and this is the screen where you notice
       the first one. Marking it here rather than on the card's own page is the whole point: you are
       already looking at the set, and the alternative was open the card, mark it, come back.

       Its own target, like the info corner: a tap on the tile means "I own one more", so wanting
       cannot also be a tap on the tile. Hidden while a sweep is armed for the same reason info is
       -- a button would swallow the start of a drag. *@
    @if (OnWant.HasDelegate && ShowInfo)
    {
        <button class="want @(Wanted > 0 ? "on" : "")" type="button"
                @onclick="Want" @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                @onpointerdown:stopPropagation="true"
                @oncontextmenu:stopPropagation="true"
                tabindex="-1"
                aria-pressed="@Aria.Flag(Wanted > 0)"
                aria-label="@(Wanted > 0 ? $"On your wishlist: {Card.Name}" : $"Add {Card.Name} to your wishlist")"
                title="@(Wanted > 0 ? "On your wishlist" : "Add to your wishlist")">
            @* Drawn, not typed: the heart characters that carry a filled and an outlined form are
               not in every system font, and the pair that are do not match each other's weight. *@
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="11" height="11" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                <path d="M8 14S1.6 9.8 1.6 5.9A3.4 3.4 0 0 1 8 4.3a3.4 3.4 0 0 1 6.4 1.6C14.4 9.8 8 14 8 14z"
                      fill="@(Wanted > 0 ? "currentColor" : "none")"
                      stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            </svg>
        </button>
    }

    @if (Count > 0)
    {
        <span class="cnt">@Count</span>
    }
    @* Distinct from the owned count: this is "I just pulled this" rather than "I own this". *@
    @if (Picked > 0)
    {
        <span class="pick">@(Picked > 1 ? $"+{Picked}" : "+")</span>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public PocketCard Card { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public int Index { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Element id, so the grid's aria-activedescendant can point at this tile.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? DomId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The accessible name. Built by CardGrid so every surface says the same thing.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Label { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public int Count { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Copies picked in the current action, e.g. cards pulled from a pack being logged.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int Picked { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this card has earned its gold flair. Passed in rather than derived here: the rule
    /// needs the rarity ladder, and the tile knows only its own card.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Gold { get; set; }
    /// <param name="Invert">
    /// Do the opposite of the grid's current mode — shift-click or right-click. The tile does not
    /// know whether that means add or remove; the grid owns the mode.
    /// </param>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<(int Index, bool Invert)> OnActivate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// False while a sweep is armed. Both affordances would fight the sweep: the button would
    /// swallow the start of a drag, and a slow drag would read as a long press.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowInfo { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Raised on a long press. The page navigates; the tile only detects.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<PocketCard> OnHold { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Copies wanted on the wishlist the page is pointing at. Zero draws an empty heart.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int Wanted { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Toggle the card on that wishlist. Absent where the page has no wishlist to write to, which
    /// is what hides the heart — the log-a-pack grid, for one, where a tap means something else
    /// entirely.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<PocketCard> OnWant { get; set; }

    private Task Want() => OnWant.InvokeAsync(Card);

    private PocketCard? _lastCard;
    private int _lastCount = -1;
    private int _lastIndex = -1;
    private int _lastPicked = -1;
    private bool _lastShowInfo = true;
    private bool _lastGold;
    private int _lastWanted = -1;

    protected override bool ShouldRender()
    {
        if (ReferenceEquals(_lastCard, Card) && _lastCount == Count
            && _lastIndex == Index && _lastPicked == Picked && _lastShowInfo == ShowInfo
            && _lastGold == Gold && _lastWanted == Wanted)
            return false;

        _lastCard = Card;
        _lastCount = Count;
        _lastIndex = Index;
        _lastPicked = Picked;
        _lastShowInfo = ShowInfo;
        _lastGold = Gold;
        _lastWanted = Wanted;
        return true;
    }

    /// <summary>Caption for the hover art: enough to identify the card without reading the image.</summary>
    private string Tip => $"{Card.Name}\n{Card.Set} {Card.Number} · {Card.Rarity}";

    // ---- long press --------------------------------------------------------------------

    private const int HoldMs = 450;
    private const double MoveTolerance = 10;

    private CancellationTokenSource? _hold;
    private bool _held;
    private double _downX;
    private double _downY;

    private async Task PointerDown(PointerEventArgs e)
    {
        if (!ShowInfo || e.Button != 0) return;

        _held = false;
        _downX = e.ClientX;
        _downY = e.ClientY;

        _hold?.Cancel();
        _hold = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _hold.Token;

        try
        {
            await Task.Delay(HoldMs, token);
        }
        catch (TaskCanceledException)
        {
            return;
        }

        // The tap that follows must not also add a copy, so the flag is checked in OnClick.
        _held = true;
        await OnHold.InvokeAsync(Card);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A finger that travels is scrolling, not holding. Without this, any slow scroll that began
    /// on a tile would open card detail.
    /// </summary>
    private void PointerMove(PointerEventArgs e)
    {
        if (_hold is null || _hold.IsCancellationRequested) return;

        if (Math.Abs(e.ClientX - _downX) > MoveTolerance || Math.Abs(e.ClientY - _downY) > MoveTolerance)
            CancelHold();
    }

    private void PointerUp() => _hold?.Cancel();

    private void CancelHold() => _hold?.Cancel();

    private Task OnClick(MouseEventArgs e)
    {
        // Swallowed exactly once: the click that ends a long press would otherwise increment the
        // count on the way out of navigating away.
        if (_held)
        {
            _held = false;
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        return OnActivate.InvokeAsync((Index, e.ShiftKey));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Right-click means the opposite of a left-click, exactly as shift-click does. It does not
    /// consult <c>_held</c>: a long press is a touch gesture and cannot produce a right-click.
    /// </summary>
    private Task RightClick(MouseEventArgs e) => OnActivate.InvokeAsync((Index, true));
}
